@meta
id: 30-06
title: Two things with the same name
chapter: 30
type: lesson
skills: ctags
par: 40
minutes: 6
vscode: F12 opens a peek list when there is more than one result
vimrc: set hidden
open: src/domain/order/order.provider.ts

@brief
Two classes in this tree have a method called `refund`. That is normal —
`findOne`, `create`, `handle` and `refund` are everywhere in a real
codebase, and a tags file indexes names, not types. It cannot know which
one you meant.

`Ctrl-]` takes the first match and says nothing. Sometimes that is the
one you wanted. Here it is not.

    PAY-311  PaymentService.refund compares the gateway status to the
             string '200'. It is a number. Every refund is reported as
             failed.

Fix the comparison in the payment service. `OrderProvider.refund` is
fine and must not change.

@keys
| `g]` | like `Ctrl-]`, but list the matches and ask which one |
| `:tselect name` | the same list, by name |
| `:tjump name` | jump if there is one match, list if there are several |
| `Ctrl-W g]` | pick from the list, open it in a split |
| `:tnext` `:tprevious` | step through the matches without the list |

@hints
Put the cursor on `refund` in the call inside `cancel`, then `g]`
instead of `Ctrl-]`.
--
The list shows both definitions with their file and the line that
defines them. Type the number you want and press Enter.
--
`/refund(order` `Enter`, `g]`, `2` `Enter`. Then `/'200'` `Enter` and
take the two quotes off.

@solution
    /refund(order<Enter>  g]  2<Enter>
    /'200'<Enter>  x  f' x  :wqa<Enter>

`g]` is the one to reach for by default. It costs one extra keystroke
when there is a single match and it saves you from silently editing the
wrong `create` when there are nine.

`:tjump` — and `g Ctrl-]`, its cursor-word form — splits the difference:
straight there when the name is unique, a list when it is not. Plenty of
people map `Ctrl-]` to `g Ctrl-]` and never think about it again.

This is also the first honest limit of a tags file. A language server
knows that the thing to the left of `.refund` is a `PaymentService`, so
it would have gone straight to the right definition with no list at all.
The index cannot know that, because it never type-checked anything. What
you get in exchange is that it works on any file, in any language, on
any machine, in a second — including the ones where no server exists.

The list is a reasonable price. Remember it when you get to the rename
lesson, where the same blindness costs rather more.

@start tags
!_TAG_FILE_FORMAT	2	//
!_TAG_FILE_SORTED	1	//
OrderProvider	src/domain/order/order.provider.ts	/^export class OrderProvider {$/;"	c
PaymentService	src/infrastructure/services/payment.service.ts	/^export class PaymentService {$/;"	c
cancel	src/domain/order/order.provider.ts	/^  public async cancel(id: string) {$/;"	m	class:OrderProvider
refund	src/domain/order/order.provider.ts	/^  public async refund(order: Order) {$/;"	m	class:OrderProvider
refund	src/infrastructure/services/payment.service.ts	/^  public async refund(orderId: string) {$/;"	m	class:PaymentService

@start src/domain/order/order.provider.ts
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(protected paymentService: PaymentService) {}

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    await this.refund(order);

    return order;
  }

  public async refund(order: Order) {
    if (order.total === 0) {
      return order;
    }

    return this.paymentService.refund(order.id);
  }
}

@start src/infrastructure/services/payment.service.ts
export class PaymentService {
  public async refund(orderId: string) {
    const response = await this.http.post('/refunds', { orderId });

    if (response.status !== '200') {
      throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data.receiptId;
  }
}

@expected src/infrastructure/services/payment.service.ts
export class PaymentService {
  public async refund(orderId: string) {
    const response = await this.http.post('/refunds', { orderId });

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data.receiptId;
  }
}
